Desiide: default layout
Purpose: the Desiide panel is visible on first run, in the secondary side bar (right), where users
  expect an AI panel. Users can still move, hide, or switch away from it, and that choice is kept.
  1. viewsExtensionPoint.ts: the built-in desiide-ai's view container is placed in the secondary side
     bar instead of the activity bar.
  2. workbench.contribution.ts: `workbench.secondarySideBar.defaultVisibility` defaults to `visible`,
     so the side bar also opens in empty windows. Upstream only opens it for new users when Copilot
     chat is enabled, and VSCodium disables that (`chat.disableAIFeatures`).
  3. layout.ts: a window with no saved secondary-side-bar state opens the Desiide container instead
     of upstream's default (the hidden Copilot chat container, which shows as an empty panel).
     `openViewContainer` waits for extensions to register, and falls back to upstream's default if
     desiide-ai is disabled.
Why not the extension API: desiide-ai's manifest contributes the container to `activitybar` so the
  same build works in stock and older VS Code (Beta standalone channel, `engines ^1.100`), where the
  layout must not change; only the built-in copy moves (a same-named third-party extension is
  unaffected). Extension `configurationDefaults` load after the workbench layout is computed, and no
  API picks the side bar's initial container, so neither can be done from the extension.
Upstream files: src/vs/workbench/api/browser/viewsExtensionPoint.ts,
  src/vs/workbench/browser/workbench.contribution.ts, src/vs/workbench/browser/layout.ts

